.SH NAME
goblin-skiff \- mobile shell with roaming and intelligent local echo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B goblin-skiff
.RI [ options ]
[--]
[user@]host
[command...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Goblin Skiff is a fork of Mosh, optimized for bandwidth-constrained links.
.PP
\fBgoblin-skiff\fP (mobile shell) is a remote terminal application that
supports intermittent connectivity, allows roaming, and provides
speculative local echo and line editing of user keystrokes.

Compared with \fBssh\fP, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP is more robust \(em its
connections stay up across sleeps and changes in the client's IP
address \(em and more responsive, because the protocol is tolerant of
packet loss and the client can echo most keystrokes immediately,
without waiting for a network round-trip.

\fBgoblin-skiff\fP uses \fBssh\fP to establish a connection to the remote host
and authenticate with existing means (e.g., public-key authentication
or a password). \fBgoblin-skiff\fP executes the unprivileged
\fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP
helper program on the server, then closes the SSH connection and
starts the \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP, which establishes a long-lived datagram
connection over UDP.

The wrapper displays both endpoint release versions on connection. Goblin
1.4.0-goblin20260915.1 establishes compatibility protocol 1; compatible
releases may differ and continue to negotiate optional features separately.
Different Goblin protocols are rejected before launching the client or jump
relays. Earlier endpoints are labelled unversioned and retain existing feature
fallbacks, without a compatibility guarantee. Use \-\-version to inspect the
local release and build identifier.

The wrapper passes the client's \fBTERM\fP value to the remote login session.
This lets remote applications detect client capabilities such as the Kitty
graphics protocol advertised by \fBxterm\-ghostty\fP. The corresponding
terminfo entry must be installed on the remote host.

To improve responsiveness, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP runs a predictive model of the
server's behavior in the background, trying to guess the effect of
each keystroke on the screen. It makes predictions for normal typing,
backspace, and the left- and right-arrow keys. When it is confident,
\fBgoblin-skiff\fP displays the predictions without waiting for the
server. The predictive model must prove itself anew on each row of the
terminal and after each control character, so \fBgoblin-skiff\fP avoids
echoing passwords or non-echoing editor commands.

By default, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP shows its predictions only on high-latency
connections and to smooth out network glitches. (On longer-latency
links, the predicted cells are underlined until confirmed by the
server.) Occasional echo mistakes are corrected within a network
round-trip and do not cause lasting effect.

\fBgoblin-skiff\fP does not support sshfs. This build includes experimental
stream forwarding for local TCP forwards, remote TCP forwards, SOCKS5
dynamic forwards, SSH agent forwarding, and basic X11 forwarding.
\fBgoblin-skiff\fP works through typical client-side network address
translators but requires UDP to pass between client and server. By
default, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP uses the ports between 60000 and 61000, but allows
the user to request a particular UDP port instead.

Currently, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP has limited support for IPv6, dual-stack
networks, and servers with multiple addresses.  At session start, it
will select a single IPv4 or IPv6 server address to connect to for the
lifetime of the session.

\fBgoblin-skiff\fP will do its best to arrange a UTF-8 character set locale on
the client and server. The client must have locale-related environment
variables that specify UTF-8. \fBgoblin-skiff\fP will pass these client
variables to the \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP on its command line, but in most
cases they will not need to be used. \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP first attempts
to use its own locale-related environment variables, which come from
the system default configuration (sometimes /etc/default/locale) or
from having been passed over the SSH connection. But if these
variables don't call for the use of UTF-8, \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP will
apply the locale-related environment variables from the client and try
again.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-socks5\-proxy=\fIHOST:PORT\fP
Use a SOCKS5 proxy for both SSH CONNECT and session UDP ASSOCIATE.
Useful with tailscaled \-\-tun=userspace-networking on devices without TUN
or kernel modules. Destination names are resolved by the proxy; only the
proxy address is resolved locally. IPv6 proxies use [ADDRESS]:PORT.
The proxy connection's address family is independent of \-\-family;
use a numeric destination to constrain proxy-side IPv4/IPv6 selection.
There is no direct fallback. The proxy must support no-authentication mode,
UDP ASSOCIATE, and numeric UDP relay bind addresses. SOCKS fragmentation
and proxy username/password authentication are not supported.

SSH authentication, host-key checks and host settings remain in effect.
This explicit option replaces ProxyCommand and disables SSH connection
sharing. Discovered ProxyJump routes still apply: SSH follows the complete
route, and session UDP reaches the first relay through the proxy.
Subsequent hops require normal UDP reachability. Not compatible with
\-\-local; overrides \-\-experimental-remote-ip destination discovery.

The association is recreated after proxy disconnection without resetting
session keys or screen state. Datagrams are not queued while disconnected.
An idle association adds no periodic heartbeat beyond normal Skiff
keepalives. Tailscale's own background traffic is outside Skiff's accounting.
This is distinct from \-D, which provides TCP-only dynamic forwarding.

.nf
goblin-skiff \-\-socks5-proxy=127.0.0.1:1055 user@host
.fi

.TP
.B \-J \fI[user@]host[:port][,...]\fP, \-\-jump=\fIHOSTS\fP
Route both SSH setup and the encrypted Skiff UDP session through one to four
jump hosts. OpenSSH ProxyJump configuration (including nested first-hop
aliases and \-J in \-\-ssh) is also discovered. The updated goblin-skiff-server
must be installed on every jump and the destination. The client requires UDP
reachability only to the first jump; each jump must reach the next over UDP.
No SSH connection remains in the data path. This is not arbitrary UDP port
forwarding; \-L/\-R and SOCKS5 \-D remain TCP-only.

Use each jump's SSH Host configuration for its credentials and ports, or
specify user/port in the jump list. Bracket IPv6 addresses. The \-F file from
\-\-ssh is carried to jump connections; destination-specific \-i/\-l/\-p
options are not. Custom \-\-ssh commands must support OpenSSH \-G.
Arbitrary ProxyCommand programs are not translated to UDP relay routes.

Each relay uses an independent authentication key and sees only the inner
encrypted Skiff packets, not the terminal session key. Client address changes
are accepted only after a newer authenticated packet. Outer framing adds
24 bytes per hop, or 36 bytes with \-\-fips-crypto; both endpoints budget for
this overhead. FIPS mode must be available on every jump too.

.TP
.B \-\-jump\-server=\fICOMMAND\fP
Server command on all jump hosts (default: goblin-skiff-server).
Separate from \-\-server on the final destination.

.TP
.B \-\-jump\-port=\fIPORT[:PORT2]\fP
UDP listener range on all jumps (default: 60001:60999). The existing \-p
still selects the final destination's UDP port. Each relay binds the incoming
SSH interface. For a jump behind NAT, forward the selected UDP port without
changing its number. Port zero requests an OS-assigned port.

.TP
.B \-\-jump\-idle\-timeout=\fISECONDS\fP
Expire a relay after this much authenticated client silence (default: 86400,
allowed range: 1800 through 604800). Normal keepalives traverse the relay;
there is no additional periodic heartbeat. Normal client exit sends
best-effort authenticated closes. Lost closes are bounded by the idle lease.
Unused relays and relay-mode destination setup expire after ten minutes.
Reconnecting after relay expiry requires a new session.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-kitty, \-\-no\-sixel
Treat the named graphics protocol as unsupported by the local terminal.
Disable its startup mascot probes and output. Disabling Kitty also suppresses
remote Kitty image rendering locally. Both options together select an ASCII
mascot. These options override forced mascot formats, which fall back to
ASCII, without changing the remote TERM value.

.TP
.B \-\-mascot=\fIFORMAT\fP, \-\-no\-mascot
Select auto, kitty, sixel, ascii, or none for the client-local startup mascot.
The mascot is printed once as inline output. In native-scrollback mode, the
remote output gradually scrolls earlier output into history as it needs more
rows, rather than hiding the mascot immediately on connection. Full-screen
applications, mouse reporting, graphics and explicit repaints release the
remaining space.
Retention of graphics in history depends on the local terminal.

.PP
Options named \fB \-\-experimental-*\fP are subject to change or
removal in future versions of Skiff; their design or function is not
yet final.

.TP
.B \fIcommand\fP
Command to run on remote host. By default, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP executes a login shell.

.TP
.B \-\-lossy=\fIQUALITY\fP
Use lossy WebP for image transport, with an integer quality from 0 to 100.
Higher values preserve more detail; 100 is still lossy. The default is
lossless WebP. Images with at most 16 RGBA colors use an exact palette and
DjVuLibre cjb2 when the client supports it. This option does not enable
lossy DjVu encoding.

.TP
.B \-\-djvu\-lossy
Allow cjb2 lossy encoding for two-color images. This can remove small marks
and substitute similar shapes, including text characters. The default is
lossless. Images with three to sixteen colors remain lossless. This flag
is independent of \fB\-\-lossy\fP and does not affect WebP quality.

.TP
.B \-\-client=\fIPATH\fP
path to client helper on local machine (default: "goblin-skiff-client")

.TP
.B \-\-server=\fICOMMAND\fP
command to run server helper on remote machine (default: "goblin-skiff-server")

The server helper is unprivileged and can be installed in the user's
home directory.

This option can be used to set environment variables for the server by
using the
.BR env (1)
command to wrap the actual server command.  See
.BR goblin-skiff-server (1)
for available environment variables.

.TP
.B \-\-tmux\-control
Negotiate reliable passthrough for \fBtmux -CC\fP integration with the local
terminal (for example, iTerm2). Both endpoints must support this feature.
Run \fBgoblin-skiff --tmux-control host -- tmux -CC new-session -A -s main\fP,
or launch \fBtmux -CC\fP later from the remote shell. The unframed \fB-C\fP
interface is not supported by this option.

The control protocol uses the existing encrypted and compressed transport.
All bytes must be delivered, which can use more bandwidth than screen diffs.
Pending queues apply backpressure at 64 KiB plus at most one PTY read.
Prediction, status overlays, and Skiff escape-key shortcuts are suspended during
control mode; detach using the local terminal's tmux integration. Normal screen
rendering resumes after detach. Temporary network loss and roaming remain
supported; restarting the Skiff client requires a new tmux attachment.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-downloads
Disable all routes of the Goblin inline download extension, including parent
terminal probes. When enabled, OSC 777 goblin-download requests are forwarded
to a supporting Goblin parent terminal, otherwise translated to Kitty file
transfer if supported, otherwise an automatic prompt asks to save to local
Downloads. Press y to select Save, then Enter after the confirmation appears.
Enter defaults to declining; n always declines. The Skiff escape prefix followed
by 0 (normally Ctrl-^ then 0) hides or reopens the
pending offer. Pasted text cannot approve a download.
Forwarding creates no intermediate copy; the parent handles permission and
storage. A parent refusal is final, not a trigger for another save prompt.
This requires support from both Skiff endpoints. Files use binary zstd level
22 compression over the background FEC path, below terminal and socket traffic.
The local fallback never overwrites existing files. Kitty owns its filesystem
semantics; reported collisions are given a suffix before payload is sent.
Downloaded content is never executed.
The maximum file size and aggregate active download size is 64 MiB.

.TP
.B \-\-download\-directory=\fIDIR\fP
Select the existing local fallback download directory (default: ~/Downloads).
The local MOSH_DOWNLOAD_DIR environment variable also selects this directory.
This option does not change the directory on a parent terminal's host.
Completed local fallback downloads are permanent; incomplete
files are removed on normal cancellation or session closure.

.TP
.B \-\-fips\-crypto
Require the client and server to negotiate the \fBaes128-gcm-v1\fP transport
suite during setup. This suite uses AES-128-GCM, HKDF-SHA-256, and random
generation from a configured OpenSSL 3 FIPS provider. Setup fails rather than
falling back if either endpoint cannot initialize that provider or the server
does not confirm the suite. Provider-generated GCM IVs add 12 bytes to each UDP
datagram compared with the default OCB framing.

This mode is a building block for a future FIPS-compliant deployment; it is not
by itself a claim that \fBgoblin-skiff\fP or the surrounding system is FIPS
compliant. The OpenSSL module and configuration, entropy source, SSH bootstrap,
operating environment, and operational controls must be assessed separately.

.TP
.B \-\-ssh=\fICOMMAND\fP
OpenSSH command to remotely execute goblin-skiff-server on the remote machine
(default: "ssh")

An alternate ssh port can be specified with, \fIe.g.\fP, \-\-ssh="ssh \-p 2222".

.TP
.B \-\-ssh-pty\fP
.B \-\-no-ssh-pty\fP
Enable or disable ssh's use of a pty when connecting to a remote host.
The default is enabled.

.TP
.B \-\-predict=\fIWHEN\fP
Controls use of speculative local echo. WHEN defaults to `adaptive'
(show predictions on slower links and to smooth out network glitches)
and can also be `always` or `never'.

The MOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY environment variable controls this setting
permanently and can adopt the same three values.

Even on `always', \fBgoblin-skiff\fP will only show predictions when it is
confident. This generally means a previous prediction on the same row
of the terminal has been confirmed by the server, without any
intervening control character keystrokes.

.TP
.B \-a
Synonym for \-\-predict=always

.TP
.B \-n
Synonym for \-\-predict=never

.TP
.B \-\-predict\-overwrite\fP
When prediction is enabled, do not insert speculative local echo
before existing text, but overwrite it instead.

The MOSH_PREDICTION_OVERWRITE environment variable also enables this
if its value is 'yes'.

.TP
.B \-o
Synonym for \-\-predict\-overwrite

.TP
.B \-\-family=inet
Only use IPv4 for the SSH connection and Skiff session.

.TP
.B \-\-family=inet6
Only use IPv6 for the SSH connection and Skiff session.  This and the
following modes require Perl's IO::Socket::IP or IO::Socket::INET6
modules.

.TP
.B \-\-family=auto
Autodetect IPv4 or IPv6 for hosts that only have addresses
in a single family.  Hosts with both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses will
raise an error, and require re-invocation of \fBgoblin-skiff\fP with another
\fB\-\-family\fP option.

.TP
.B \-\-family=all
Choose an address from all available IPv4 or IPv6 address, even for
dual-stack hosts.  This is the most convenient option, but requires
dual-stack connectivity, and Mosh 1.2.5 or later on the server, when
roaming with dual-stack servers.

.TP
.B \-\-family=prefer-inet
Similar to \fB\-\-family=all\fP, but attempt connects to the IPv4
addresses first.  This is the default.

.TP
.B \-\-family=prefer-inet6
Similar to \fB\-\-family=all\fP, but attempt connects to the IPv6
addresses first.

.TP
.B \-4
Synonym for \-\-family=inet

.TP
.B \-6
Synonym for \-\-family=inet6

.TP
.B \-p \fIPORT\fP[:\fIPORT2\fP], \-\-port=\fIPORT\fP[:\fIPORT2\fP]
Use a particular server-side UDP port or port range,
for example, if this is the
only port that is forwarded through a firewall to the
server. With \fB\-p 0\fP, the server will let the operating system pick an
available UDP port. Otherwise, \fBgoblin-skiff\fP will choose a port between 60000 and
61000. Please note that this option does not affect the server-side
port used by SSH.

.TP
.B \-L [\fIBIND\fP:]\fIPORT\fP:\fIHOST\fP:\fIHOSTPORT\fP
Forward a local TCP port to the remote side of the Skiff session. This
is similar to OpenSSH's
.B \-L
option: connections accepted on the client are carried over the Skiff
UDP connection and opened from the server side to
\fIHOST\fP:\fIHOSTPORT\fP.

.TP
.B \-R [\fIBIND\fP:]\fIPORT\fP:\fIHOST\fP:\fIHOSTPORT\fP
Forward a remote TCP port to the local side of the Skiff session. This
is similar to OpenSSH's
.B \-R
option: connections accepted by \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP are carried over the
Skiff UDP connection and opened from the client side to
\fIHOST\fP:\fIHOSTPORT\fP.

.TP
.B \-D [\fIBIND\fP:]\fIPORT\fP
Open a local SOCKS5 dynamic forward. SOCKS connections accepted on the
client are carried over the Skiff UDP connection and opened from the
server side.

.TP
.B \-A
Forward the local SSH authentication agent to the remote login
session. The forwarded agent socket is exposed through
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
inside the remote session.

.TP
.B \-X
Forward X11 connections from the remote login session to the client's
local X server. The remote session receives a forwarded
.B DISPLAY
and a temporary
.B XAUTHORITY
file. This is basic trusted X11 forwarding; it does not implement
OpenSSH's untrusted X11 security extension handling. The client must
have MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 data for its local
.B DISPLAY
in
.B XAUTHORITY
or
.IR ~/.Xauthority .

.TP
.B \-\-stream\-delay=\fIMS\fP
Coalesce forwarded stream bytes for up to \fIMS\fP milliseconds before
sending a Skiff datagram. The default is 75 ms. Raising this value can
reduce packet overhead on very slow links; lowering it can improve
latency for interactive forwarded connections.

.TP
.B \-\-stream\-bandwidth=\fIBPS\fP
Cap forwarded stream payload bytes per second. By default, supporting peers
adapt forwarding to the independently estimated outgoing link budget;
older peers use 2048 bytes per second. An explicit ceiling applies to
forwarding, not terminal state updates. The shared session pacer still
accounts for terminal, forwarding and bulk traffic together.

.PP
Terminal state compression is a core part of this branch's
low-bandwidth strategy. When both peers advertise zstd support, every
serialized terminal state update uses zstd level 22. There is no size
threshold or zlib comparison. Initial packets remain zlib-compressed
so older peers remain compatible.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-zstd\-dict=\fIFILE\fP
Use \fIFILE\fP as a zstd dictionary for terminal state updates. The
dictionary file is normally produced by
.BR goblin-skiff-compile-dictionary (1)
and stored compressed with zstd level 22. The wrapper uploads the
compressed dictionary file to the server over SSH during setup. The
server decompresses it, uses it for this session, and removes the
uploaded copy; there is no server-side dictionary cache. Dictionary
compression is used only when both peers advertise the same dictionary
id.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-sample\-log=\fIFILE\fP
Write received, uncompressed terminal state samples to \fIFILE\fP in a
zstd-compressed framed format suitable for
.BR goblin-skiff-compile-dictionary (1).
This file can contain terminal contents and should be treated as
sensitive.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-sample\-min\-size=\fIBYTES\fP
Only log received state samples at least this large. The default is 0.

Terminal screen updates and keystrokes are highest priority. X11 and
SSH agent forwarding are medium priority and use a separate stream
token bucket from local, remote, and SOCKS5 TCP forwarding, which are
low priority. Skiff emits at most one forwarded stream event per
reliable send opportunity and caps forwarded stream chunks below the
path-MTU payload budget. \fBgoblin-skiffcp\fP bulk datagrams are sent only
when no reliable terminal or stream-forwarding data is queued.

.PP
Sustained terminal state updates are paced between 10 frames per
second on low-RTT paths and 4 frames per second on high-RTT paths. The
first update after an idle period is not held for a full frame
interval. After 15 seconds without traffic, clients and servers that
negotiate \fBkeepalive-v1\fP use an authenticated connection-layer ping
and pong. Continued inactivity backs the interval off through 15
seconds, 30 seconds, 1, 2, 4, 8, and 15 minutes. Real terminal,
forwarding, or bulk traffic resets it to 15 seconds. These keepalives do
not carry a serialized terminal state, compression frame, fragment
header, or chaff. Mixed-version connections retain the legacy state
heartbeat.

.TP
.B \-\-bind\-server={ssh|any|\fIIP\fP}
Control the IP address that the \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP binds to.

The default is `ssh', in which case the server will reply from the IP
address that the SSH connection came from (as found in the
\fBSSH_CONNECTION\fP environment variable). This is useful for
multihomed servers.

With \-\-bind\-server=any, the server will reply on the default interface
and will not bind to a particular IP address. This can be useful if
the connection is made through \fBsslh\fP or another tool that makes
the SSH connection appear to come from localhost.

With \-\-bind\-server=\fIIP\fP, the server will attempt to bind to the
specified IP address.

.TP
.B \-\-native\-scroll
Keep the session on the client's primary screen so upward screen motion is
recorded by the local terminal emulator's native scrollback. This is the
default. The scrollback contains the screen states that the client actually
receives; output from intermediate states skipped on a slow or disconnected
link cannot be reconstructed.

.TP
.B \-\-alternate\-screen
Send the \fBsmcup\fP and \fBrmcup\fP terminal initialization strings, isolating
the session in the alternate screen. This restores the traditional Mosh
behavior but usually disables the local terminal's native scrollback.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-init
Compatibility alias for \fB\-\-native\-scroll\fP. The inverse \fB\-\-init\fP
is a compatibility alias for \fB\-\-alternate\-screen\fP.

.TP
.B \-\-local
Invoke \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP locally, without using \fBssh\fP.  This
option requires the \fBhost\fP argument to be a local, numeric
IPv4/IPv6 address.  This option is useful for testing.

.TP
.B \-\-experimental\-remote\-ip={proxy|local|remote}
Select the method used to discover the IP address that the
\fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP connects to.

The default is \fBproxy\fP, which uses SSH's
.B \-\-ssh\-proxy\-command
option to generate and report the exact address that \fBssh\fP uses to
connect to the remote host.  This option is generally the most
compatible with hosts and other options configured in \fBssh\fP
configuration files.  However, this may not work for some
configurations, or for environments where a \fBssh\fP bastion host
forwards to a remote machine.  It only works with \fBOpenSSH\fP.

With \fBremote\fP, the server's
.B SSH_CONNECTION
environment variable will be used.  This is useful for environments
where \fBssh\fP forwarding is used, or the
.B \-\-ssh\-proxy\-command
option is used for other purposes.

With \fBlocal\fP, Skiff resolves the hostname given on its command
line, and uses that address for both \fBssh\fP and Skiff connections.
This option ignores any configuration in
.B ssh_config
for the same hostname.

.SH ESCAPE SEQUENCES

The default escape character used by Skiff is ASCII RS (decimal 30).
This is typically typed as \fBCtrl-^\fP or \fBCtrl-Shift-6\fP, on US
English keyboards.  Users of non-English keyboards may find it
difficult or impossible to type the default escape character, and may
need to change the escape character.  See the description of
MOSH_ESCAPE_KEY, below.  In this description, the configured escape
character is represented as \fBEsc\fP.

There are two slightly different modes for escape sequences, depending
whether the escape character is printable or not.

If the escape character is a printable character, it must be prefixed
with a newline, similar to \fBOpenSSH\fP.  To send the escape character
itself, type it twice.  If the escape character is set to \fB~\fP,
\fBgoblin-skiff\fP will behave much like \fBOpenSSH\fP.

If the escape character is a non-printable control character, no
prefix is used and the escape character is recognized at any time.  To
send the escape character itself, type the escape character, then its
corresponding ASCII character (for \fBCtrl-^\fP you would type \fB^\fP,
for \fBCtrl-B\fP you would type \fBB\fP).

The escape sequence to shut down the connection is
\fBEsc .\fP. The sequence \fBEsc Ctrl-Z\fP suspends the client.
\fBEsc 0\fP toggles the local control panel, preserving its directories,
selections, and pending requests while hidden. With the default prefix,
type Ctrl-6 in Kitty (often Ctrl-Shift-6 in other terminals), release it,
then press 0. The command also works while
the panel is visible. Bracketed paste cannot invoke local commands.
Alt-0 is not reserved by the client.
Any other sequence passes both characters through to the server.
.SS Live file browser
Tab or Shift-Tab changes pane; Up/Down moves selection; Page Up/Down scroll
a viewport. Left or Backspace opens the parent directory; Right enters a
directory and does nothing on files; r refreshes. Reversing Up/Down moves the
highlight immediately; the list scrolls only at the viewport edges. Each
pane preserves its scroll position while switching sides or hiding the panel.
The Unicode-bordered panes show file sizes in alphabetical order, with ASCII
case ignored and original bytes breaking ties. Each host scans and sorts its
own directory names inside a killable worker; only small windows cross the
link. Non-ASCII names use byte ordering, not locale collation. There is no
visible pagination. The sorted-name cache is limited to one million names or
an estimated 64 MiB; slow scans do not block the terminal loop.
At most eight 64-entry windows are cached. Both endpoints check the watched
window every three seconds while open. Unchanged checks consume no network
traffic; changed sizes send metadata deltas. Added or removed names renew
the first window and invalidate old directory cookies. A deep scroll position
therefore restarts on membership changes. Hidden browsing state is preserved,
and watches resume when reopened. The remote terminal continues underneath.
.SS Copying and synchronizing
Enter, F5 or c copies the selected file or entire directory into the other pane.
The transfer is queued immediately and the browser stays visible; there is
no confirmation screen. Matching files are updated. x cancels the active
transfer and queue. Bracketed paste and Kitty repeat/release events cannot
start a copy. Remote-initiated Downloads offers still require permission.
Existing regular files are synchronized with librsync: signatures travel
from destination to source, then deltas travel back. Both directions use
zstd-22 and the background FEC lane, with interactive acknowledgments.
Large deltas fall back to sending the complete file. Reconstructed files
are SHA-256 verified before atomic publication. Detected concurrent changes
to an existing destination fail the transfer; new-file collisions never
overwrite.
Folder copies merge recursively, including empty directories. They never
delete extra destination files. Symlinks and special files are rejected.
Regular-file permissions (not special bits) are copied; timestamps, ownership,
directory modes and hard-link relationships are not preserved.
The popup shows progress and up to eight waiting jobs. x cancels the active
job and queue, keeping completed files. Hiding/reopening the popup or a
temporary
disconnect preserves transfer state; exiting the session does not.
Unsent data is discarded on cancel; the bounded in-flight FEC window can
still drain before another job starts.
Limits are 64 GiB per file, one million entries and 64 path components.
Both endpoints need a build with librsync 2.3+ and OpenSSL 3. The capability
is negotiated once as file-sync-v1. Configure with --with-librsync to require
support, or --without-librsync for browsing only. This does not change the
separate goblin-skiffcp command protocol.
.SS Adaptive link pacing
Supporting peers negotiate independent outgoing send budgets once at setup.
Each direction starts conservatively at 240 IP bytes/second and adjusts using
authenticated delivery, loss and round-trip feedback from real traffic.
There is no padding speed test or idle measurement traffic. The panel shows
observed traffic and send budget separately for upload and download, in kbit/s,
and outgoing loss. Sparse traffic cannot establish unused link capacity.
Estimates are not guarantees; congestion probing may still lose packets.
Terminal, forwarding and bulk packets share this byte-based pacer, with
bounded foreground borrowing and lower priority for background transfers.
Pending screen changes that are waiting for their frame timer do not block
paced file traffic. Due foreground work always runs first.
Clean, busy links can double their budget per feedback round (at least
100 ms or one RTT); congestion switches to gentler growth. Clear slow-path
evidence cuts toward measured delivery speed. At most 512 background packets
can be outstanding while waiting for feedback. Scheduling slack is bounded
to 1 ms or 4 KiB and never accumulates during idle time. File-menu transfers
use this pacer instead of their legacy fixed 20 ms per-symbol delay.
Feedback itself is capped to a quarter of the outgoing budget, so a fast
incoming stream cannot crowd out keyboard input on a slow reverse path.
The stream-bandwidth option remains a manual forwarding ceiling; otherwise
forwarding follows its local directional estimate. The goblin-skiffcp rate
option remains an additional ceiling; rate=0 still obeys session pacing.
Older peers retain their existing transport behavior and non-watching browser.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
These variables configure the client, server, or both. They are
described here for ease of use.

.TP
.B MOSH_ESCAPE_KEY
When set, this configures the escape character used for local
commands.  The escape character may be set to any ASCII character in
the range 1-127.  The variable must be set with a single literal ASCII
character.  Control characters are set with the actual ASCII
control character, not with a printable representation such as "^B".

.TP
.B MOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY
Controls local echo as described above.  The command-line flag
overrides this variable.

.TP
.B MOSH_TITLE_NOPREFIX
When set, inhibits prepending "[goblin-skiff]" to window title.

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_DELAY
Default coalescing delay, in milliseconds, for forwarded stream
traffic. The
.B \-\-stream\-delay
command-line option overrides this value.

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_BANDWIDTH
Default payload-byte-per-second cap for forwarded stream traffic. The
.B \-\-stream\-bandwidth
command-line option overrides this value.

.SH FILE TRANSFER
\fBgoblin-skiffcp\fP transfers files over an active Skiff session using adjacent
bulk datagrams on the same encrypted and authenticated UDP connection.
Bulk datagrams are lowest priority and are scheduled only when
terminal updates and forwarded streams are idle. Oversized bulk
datagrams are rejected instead of being handed to UDP fragmentation.

Start the receiver on one side of the Skiff session and the sender on
the other:

.nf
remote$ goblin-skiffcp receive .
local$  goblin-skiffcp send --rate=2k --redundancy=20% file.bin
.fi

See
.BR goblin-skiffcp (1)
for details.

